Feature: Spark SQL to PySpark Conversion for Supplier Invoice Pipeline

  The pipeline must convert all Spark SQL logic in the fact_wf_supplier_invoice_orafin.ipynb notebook to PySpark DataFrame API code, including all referenced functions, with full functional equivalence. The solution must support Unity Catalog (purgo_databricks), schema (purgo_playground), and all referenced tables. All business logic, data type mappings, and orchestration must be preserved. Error handling, validation, and parameterization must be robust and explicit.

  Background:
    Given the Unity Catalog is "purgo_databricks"
    And the Unity Catalog schema is "purgo_playground"
    And all referenced tables exist and are accessible
    And all required widgets are provided at runtime:
      | widget_name              | required | default_value | allowed_values |
      | target_table_path        | true     | None          | string         |
      | partition                | true     | None          | string         |
      | table_format             | true     | None          | string         |
      | compression              | true     | None          | string         |
      | table_name               | true     | None          | string         |
      | unity_catalog            | true     | None          | string         |
      | environment              | true     | None          | string         |
      | project                  | true     | None          | string         |
      | load_type                | true     | None          | string         |
      | view_unity_catalog_name  | true     | None          | string         |
      | raw_unity_catalog        | true     | None          | string         |
      | raw_unity_catalog_hist   | true     | None          | string         |
      | config_unity_catalog     | true     | None          | string         |
      | edp_lkp_unity_catalog    | true     | None          | string         |
      | dims_unity_catalog       | true     | None          | string         |
    And all notebook-scoped functions from "ReusableFunctions" and "Constants" are available in PySpark
    And the control table "config_unity_catalog.cl_control_table" exists with its required schema
    And all required privileges for Unity Catalog are granted
    And the Spark configuration "spark.sql.sources.partitionOverwriteMode" is set to "dynamic"
    And the process is triggered by Databricks job or notebook run

  Scenario: Successful conversion of Spark SQL logic to PySpark DataFrame API
    Given the full business logic from the notebook is available
    And all referenced tables and columns exist with the expected schema
    And all required functions (e.g., get_basejob_url, read_control_table) are implemented in PySpark
    When the Spark SQL logic is converted to PySpark DataFrame API
    Then the resulting PySpark code must:
      And reproduce all CTEs, joins, filters, and transformations using DataFrame API
      And preserve all column data types and nullability as per target table schema
      And implement all COALESCE, CASE, and conditional logic using PySpark functions
      And handle all date formatting and casting using PySpark functions
      And parameterize all catalog, schema, and table references using widgets
      And write the final DataFrame to the correct Unity Catalog table with the specified partitioning, format, and compression
      And overwrite or append as per the process requirements
      And log job metadata (jobId, jobUrl) using the provided functions
      And update the control table entry as per business logic
      And produce output DataFrames/tables that match the original Spark SQL results
      And pass all acceptance criteria for data equivalence

  Scenario Outline: Data type and nullability mapping validation
    Given the source Spark SQL column <sql_column> with type <sql_type> and nullability <sql_nullability>
    When converting to PySpark DataFrame column
    Then the PySpark column <pyspark_column> must have type <pyspark_type> and nullability <pyspark_nullability>
    Examples:
      | sql_column           | sql_type   | sql_nullability | pyspark_column      | pyspark_type | pyspark_nullability |
      | invc_entry_period    | timestamp  | nullable        | invc_entry_period   | TimestampType| nullable           |
      | suplr_invc_nbr       | string     | nullable        | suplr_invc_nbr      | StringType    | nullable           |
      | vchr_nbr             | string     | nullable        | vchr_nbr            | StringType    | nullable           |
      | fscl_yr_nbr          | bigint     | nullable        | fscl_yr_nbr         | LongType      | nullable           |
      | invc_co_amt          | double     | nullable        | invc_co_amt         | DoubleType    | nullable           |
      | cost_centre_cd       | bigint     | nullable        | cost_centre_cd      | LongType      | nullable           |
      | invc_qty             | double     | nullable        | invc_qty            | DoubleType    | nullable           |
      | invc_txn_amt         | double     | nullable        | invc_txn_amt        | DoubleType    | nullable           |
      | inv_line_desc        | string     | nullable        | inv_line_desc       | StringType    | nullable           |

  Scenario Outline: Validation of widget parameterization
    Given the widget <widget_name> is provided at runtime
    When the PySpark code accesses the widget value
    Then the value must be non-null and of type <expected_type>
    And if the value is missing, the process must fail with error message "Missing required widget: <widget_name>"
    Examples:
      | widget_name              | expected_type |
      | target_table_path        | string        |
      | partition                | string        |
      | table_format             | string        |
      | compression              | string        |
      | table_name               | string        |
      | unity_catalog            | string        |
      | environment              | string        |
      | project                  | string        |
      | load_type                | string        |
      | view_unity_catalog_name  | string        |
      | raw_unity_catalog        | string        |
      | raw_unity_catalog_hist   | string        |
      | config_unity_catalog     | string        |
      | edp_lkp_unity_catalog    | string        |
      | dims_unity_catalog       | string        |

  Scenario: Error when required function implementation is missing
    Given a referenced function from "ReusableFunctions" or "Constants" is not implemented in PySpark
    When the PySpark code attempts to call the missing function
    Then the process must fail with error message "Missing function implementation: <function_name>"

  Scenario: Error when referenced table or column is missing
    Given a referenced table or column does not exist in Unity Catalog
    When the PySpark code attempts to access the missing table or column
    Then the process must fail with error message "Missing table or column: <table_or_column_name>"

  Scenario: Error when control table schema is missing or invalid
    Given the control table "config_unity_catalog.cl_control_table" does not exist or has an invalid schema
    When the PySpark code attempts to read or update the control table
    Then the process must fail with error message "Control table missing or invalid schema"

  Scenario: Error when Unity Catalog privileges are insufficient
    Given the user or service principal does not have required privileges on Unity Catalog or schema
    When the PySpark code attempts to read or write to a Unity Catalog table
    Then the process must fail with error message "Insufficient privileges for Unity Catalog operation"

  Scenario: Error when Spark configuration is not set correctly
    Given the Spark configuration "spark.sql.sources.partitionOverwriteMode" is not set to "dynamic"
    When the PySpark code attempts to overwrite partitioned tables
    Then the process must fail with error message "Partition overwrite mode not set to dynamic"

  Scenario: Error when external library or dependency is missing
    Given a required PySpark or external library is not installed in the Databricks cluster
    When the PySpark code attempts to import or use the library
    Then the process must fail with error message "Missing required library: <library_name>"

  Scenario Outline: Data equivalence validation between Spark SQL and PySpark outputs
    Given the Spark SQL output DataFrame <sql_df> and the PySpark output DataFrame <pyspark_df>
    When comparing the outputs for row count, column names, and data values
    Then the outputs must match exactly for:
      | validation_type | expected_result |
      | row_count       | equal           |
      | column_names    | equal           |
      | data_values     | equal           |
    Examples:
      | sql_df         | pyspark_df      |
      | df_sql         | df_pyspark      |
      | supplier_invoice_sql | supplier_invoice_pyspark |

  Scenario: Error when SQL construct is not directly mappable to PySpark
    Given the Spark SQL logic contains a construct not directly supported by PySpark DataFrame API
    When converting to PySpark
    Then the process must either:
      And implement equivalent logic using PySpark UDFs or alternative functions
      Or fail with error message "SQL construct not mappable to PySpark: <construct_description>"

  Scenario: Error when required widget value is not in allowed set
    Given a widget value <widget_value> for <widget_name> is not in the allowed set <allowed_values>
    When the PySpark code validates the widget value
    Then the process must fail with error message "Invalid widget value for <widget_name>: <widget_value>"

  Scenario: Error when process flow or output table is ambiguous
    Given the end-to-end process flow or output table is not fully specified
    When the PySpark code reaches the output stage
    Then the process must fail with error message "Ambiguous process flow or output table specification"

  Scenario: Error when required parameter is missing in dbutils.widgets.get()
    Given a required parameter is not supplied at runtime
    When the PySpark code calls dbutils.widgets.get(<parameter_name>)
    Then the process must fail with error message "Missing required parameter: <parameter_name>"

  Scenario: Error when writing to Unity Catalog table fails
    Given the PySpark DataFrame is ready to be written to a Unity Catalog table
    When the write operation fails due to schema mismatch, partitioning error, or other issue
    Then the process must fail with error message "Failed to write to Unity Catalog table: <table_name>"

  Scenario: Error when control table update fails
    Given the process attempts to update the control table entry
    When the update operation fails
    Then the process must fail with error message "Failed to update control table entry"

